{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"5c46e8e5eb7d1653","type":"group","x":-2320,"y":-1280,"width":4320,"height":7040,"color":"#b9ff38","label":"CSAPP CHAPTER 1 - THE BOOKS OVERVIEW"},
		{"id":"5863931996704eef","type":"file","file":"My Knowledge Base/Hardware Organization of a System.md","x":155,"y":-356,"width":590,"height":292},
		{"id":"08929efb7f39541e","type":"file","file":"My Knowledge Base/Hello World CSAPP.md","x":-1380,"y":319,"width":400,"height":400},
		{
			"id":"ff2997379ad2d71c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**What Chapter 1 (the \"Tour\") actually does:** it doesn't teach any one topic in depth — it previews the _entire book_ by tracing one tiny program (`hello.c`) all the way from typed source code to a running process, and uses that single walkthrough as an excuse to name-drop every major topic the rest of CSAPP will cover. It's a map of the territory before you start hiking it.\n\n**Skills/mindset it's setting you up to gain:**\n\n- **Compilation literacy** — understanding that \"gcc hello.c\" is actually four separate steps (preprocessor → compiler → assembler → linker) hiding behind one command, and why knowing this matters for debugging weird build errors.\n\n- **The \"power programmer\" mindset** — the book's own framing: most programmers treat the system as a black box; understanding what's underneath (compiler, OS, hardware) lets you write code that's faster, more correct, and easier to debug.\n\n- **Hardware-aware thinking** — knowing that buses, memory, and caches aren't abstract trivia but things that directly shape how fast your code runs (this is the seed for why your Cache Memory and Storage Hierarchy nodes exist later on the canvas).\n\n- **OS-level awareness** — that the OS is a layer sitting between your program and the hardware, managing processes, memory, and files so your program doesn't have to.\n\n- **Performance instincts** — the intro plants the idea that ignorance of the memory hierarchy silently kills performance, motivating you to care about things like cache locality later.\n\n- **Security instincts** — early exposure to why low-level bugs (like buffer overflows) exist at all, which sets up the reasoning for later security-focused chapters.\n\n- **Concurrency awareness** — a first touch on why modern programs deal with multiple threads/processes, foreshadowing later chapters on parallelism.\n\n- **Systems-as-a-whole view** — the payoff of the chapter: after reading it you should be able to describe, at a high level, what happens between \"you hit run\" and \"text appears on screen\" — which is the thread tying every other node on your canvas together.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2165,
			"y":-430,
			"width":600,
			"height":570,
			"color":"#d18400"
		},
